{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DVh7ijS7Q8ZEHZbq443ydA","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd002-sys\n     2→title: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n     6→  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n     7→  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n     8→  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n     9→  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n    10→  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n    11→  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n    12→  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n    13→\n    14→  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n    15→  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n    16→  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query that works on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n    20→  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n    21→  - G3: Provide a SIGPIPE handler that suppresses the broken-pipe error when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer.\n    22→  - G4: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n    23→\n    24→requirements:\n    25→  R1:\n    26→    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    27→    items:\n    28→      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n    29→      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n    30→      - R1.3: |\n    31→          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n    32→          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n    33→          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n    34→          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n    35→          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n    36→          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n    37→          output when stdout is redirected.\n    38→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n    39→\n    40→  R2:\n    41→    title: Extended file metadata\n    42→    items:\n    43→      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n    44→      - R2.2: |\n    45→          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n    46→\n    47→            type FileInfo struct {\n    48→                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n    49→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    50→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    51→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    52→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    53→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    54→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    55→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    56→                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    57→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n    58→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n    59→                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n    60→            }\n    61→      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n    62→      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n    63→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n    64→      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n    65→\n    66→  R3:\n    67→    title: SIGPIPE handling\n    68→    items:\n    69→      - R3.1: |\n    70→          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer. Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n    71→      - R3.2: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n    72→      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n    73→\n    74→  R4:\n    75→    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    76→    items:\n    77→      - R4.1: |\n    78→          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n    79→      - R4.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n    80→      - R4.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n    81→\n    82→non_goals:\n    83→  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n    84→  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n    85→  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n    86→  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n    87→\n    88→acceptance_criteria:\n    89→  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n    90→  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n    91→  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n    92→  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n    93→  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n    94→  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    95→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94648a56-5645-4cee-8294-383e6325475e","uuid":"fd94660e-3633-4048-85c4-9ffc2b9c1500","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/94/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml","content":"id: prd002-sys\ntitle: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n\n  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query that works on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n  - G3: Provide a SIGPIPE handler that suppresses the broken-pipe error when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer.\n  - G4: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n      - R1.3: |\n          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n          output when stdout is redirected.\n      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n\n  R2:\n    title: Extended file metadata\n    items:\n      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n      - R2.2: |\n          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n\n            type FileInfo struct {\n                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n            }\n      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n\n  R3:\n    title: SIGPIPE handling\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer. Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n      - R3.2: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n\n  R4:\n    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    items:\n      - R4.1: |\n          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n      - R4.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n      - R4.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1,"totalLines":95}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019cvyvrW23cqSKGNzMJzxD2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear understanding of the PRD and the existing code. Let me plan the implementation:\n\n1. **terminal_test.go**: Tests for `IsTerminal` and `GetWinSize`\n   - R1: Test `IsTerminal` with pipe fds (must return false)\n   - R2: Test `GetWinSize` returns error for non-terminal fds (pipes)\n\n2. **stat_test.go**: Tests for `Stat`, `Lstat`, and `FileMetadata`\n   - R3: Test `Stat` and `Lstat` populate `FileMetadata` fields correctly using temp files\n   - R4: Test `Lstat` does not follow symlinks\n\nLooking at the actual implementation in `pkg/sys/stat.go`, I see it defines `FileMetadata` with fields like ModTime, ATime, Blocks, Blksize, Dev, Ino, Nlink, Uid, and Gid, along with `Stat` and `Lstat` functions. The `terminal.go` file has the terminal-related functions I need to test.\n\nNow I'm planning out the test structure. I'll follow the table-driven test pattern from `color_test.go` using `t.Run` for organization. For the terminal tests, I can use `os.Pipe()` to get a non-TTY file descriptor. For the stat tests, I'll create temporary files and symlinks to verify the metadata fields are populated correctly and that `Lstat` properly returns symlink metadata rather than following to the target. Writing test files... Writing test cases...\n\nNow I'm thinking about how to validate the FileMetadata struct fields against what os.Stat provides, so I'll need to cross-check things like inode number, user ID, and group ID using syscall.Stat_t or by extracting the Sys() data from os.Stat results.\n\nFor the symlink test case, I'll create a file and symlink, then call the appropriate function to verify it handles symlinks correctly.\n\nNow I'm thinking through which fields I can reliably cross-check against the syscall data. I can compare `ModTime` directly with `FileInfo.ModTime()`, and then extract the `Stat_t` structure to verify `Uid`, `Gid`, `Ino`, `Nlink`, `Blocks`, `Dev`, and `Blksize`. The challenge is keeping the test platform-independent while still validating these syscall-level fields. names differ between Darwin and Linux. So I have two options:\n1. Use build tags in the test file too\n2. Cross-check against `os.Stat` values that are platform-independent\n\nActually, looking at the cross-check approach: the task says \"verify FileMetadata fields against os.Stat/os.Lstat for cross-check\". Since `os.FileInfo` only exposes `Name()`, `Size()`, `Mode()`, `ModTime()`, `IsDir()`, and `Sys()`, I'll need to use `Sys()` to get the underlying `syscall.Stat_t` for the detailed fields.\n\nBut `syscall.Stat_t` field names differ between Darwin and Linux. However, the common fields like `Uid`, `Gid`, `Blocks`, `Ino` have the same names on both platforms—it's really just the time fields that diverge with `Mtimespec` on Darwin versus `Mtim` on Linux. Looking at the actual type definitions, I can see that `Ino`, `Nlink`, `Uid`, `Gid`, `Blocks`, `Blksize`, and `Dev` are present on both systems, though their underlying types vary slightly between platforms.\n\nThe simplest approach is to call `os.Stat()`, extract the `syscall.Stat_t` via `Sys()`, then compare the extracted fields against what my `FileMetadata` struct contains. Since the code already handles platform-specific extraction in separate files, I just need to verify the results match.\n\nBut I realize that approach is circular—I'd be testing the identity function. Instead, I should validate against known facts: `Nlink` should be 1 for a new file, `Uid` and `Gid` should match `os.Getuid()` and `os.Getgid()`, `ModTime` should align with `fi.ModTime()`, and fields like `Ino`, `Dev`, and `Blocks` should be non-zero or meet expected thresholds.\n\nWriting test code... Continuing with more validation checks on the file metadata properties.\n\nNow I'm writing a test for the Lstat function when it's called on a symlink, setting up a temporary directory with a target file and a symbolic link pointing to it.\n\nThen I'm calling Stat on the symlink to get the target's metadata, followed by Lstat to get the symlink's own metadata, and verifying that their inodes differ since Lstat shouldn't follow the symlink. I'm also cross-checking the result against os.Lstat to ensure consistency.\n\nNow I'm realizing that while FileMetadata doesn't store the file mode, I can still validate the inode difference to confirm Lstat is working correctly.\n\nFor the tests, I'll focus on what's actually implemented: testing IsTerminal and GetWinSize with pipes in terminal_test.go, and in stat_test.go I'll verify that Stat and Lstat populate all the FileMetadata fields correctly, plus check that Lstat returns the symlink's own inode rather than following to the target. to keep it simple and just use `os.Getuid()` for the UID check, since it matches the effective UID in standard test environments. For the GID, I'll skip the assertion entirely since directory inheritance can vary by system. Comparing the metadata fields directly using `syscall.Stat_t` - the Uid and Gid fields match across Darwin and Linux, so I can compare them straight up. For Ino and Nlink, I need to cast them since the types differ between platforms, but both are available on both systems so the casts will work fine.\n\nFor Dev, it's trickier - Darwin uses int32 while Linux uses uint64, and the existing code already handles this by casting to uint64. I should use the same conversion in my cross-check to match what extractMetadata does.\n\nActually, the real issue is that this conversion has different semantics across platforms, so I need to either use build tags or find a platform-independent way to verify the fields. Let me think about testing against values from os.Stat directly instead.\n\nI could check fields that align with os.FileInfo methods like ModTime, verify known values like Nlink being 1 for a new file or Uid matching os